  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 244 AM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThe beginning of the forecast period remains similar with high \npressure building in from the NW behind the prior week trough. This \nwill aid in subsidence over the region, dropping dew points into the \nlow 60s during the daytime hours and upper 50s at night. A weak \nshortwave on the SW edge of the high may push a few lingering cirrus \noverhead and could provide enough low level moisture for a few \ndiurnal cumulus, but otherwise clouds will be mostly clear through \nThursday. This all said, there is fairly strong NW flow remaining \naloft, and within it, opaque smoke cover from upstream fires may \nlead to slightly hazy skies at times, for this reason cloud cover \nwill be made at least 20 percent. With 850mb temperatures remaining \nthe same over the next 48 hours, surface temperatures should respond \naccordingly; although the slightly drier near surface air may allow \nfor afternoon highs to push a bit higher with the current expected \nhighs between 84-86F. \n\nAs we approach the weekend, super-geostrophic flow over the Rockies' \nridge will help induce pressure falls over the Missouri Valley. Due \nto the placement of the jet, much of the pressure depletion will be \ndue to very strong diffluence and thus weak steering flow with a \nmajority of the ensemble members showing the mid level low becoming \ndetached from the flow aloft. While ensemble members agree on this \noverall pattern, the path and the strength of the cutoff low very \nsome due to the lack of strong dynamics. While rain looks generally \nlikely for the weekend, specifics regarding magnitude of QPF and \ntiming are uncertain, and likely will remain that way until \nensembles are able to ingest the cutoff low directly. \n\nFor next week, there are not any strong signals within the 300mb\npattern leading to fairly high uncertainty on precipitation. \nHowever, regarding temperatures, the polar jet looks to be well \ndisplaced to the north, with should allow for above average 850mb \ntemperatures and therefor some likelihood of above average surface \ntemperatures.",
